/*****************************************************************************
    jitJRt2o -- F2's ORACLE, IN ITS OWN PROCESS, BECAUSE A POST-JIT INTERPRETED
    RUN IS NOT A CLEAN ORACLE FOR A RETURNED VALUE. 2026-08-05.

    THE BODY IS jitJRt2's, CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER. Nothing here calls testing()
    or jitRefire(), so the interpreter is the only engine that runs and the 120
    it produces is uncontaminated.

    ⚠ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, and it is worth knowing before someone
    "simplifies" it back into jitJRt2: the same interpreted call placed BELOW the
    jitted fires returns 5 -- ftAcc's value at the outermost activation before it
    recursed, which is the save/restore signature. Measured both ways the same
    day. jitJR does the same thing and is correct, because it reads its
    accumulator as a FIELD; the divergence only appears for a RETURNED value,
    which is why nothing had caught it before item 2 made returned values
    assertable at all. `recursive` is cleared at run time by restoreLocalFields
    (GroupActions.rtn:587), so whether the frame bracket ran depends on
    invocation history -- interpreter-side, out of item 2's scope, reported.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, exactly ONE stop() (rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;
register(JitLadder);
define
    ftN;
    ftAcc;
    ftOut;
    ft code={
        if ftN > 1;
            ftAcc = ftAcc * ftN;
            ftN = ftN - 1;
            ft();
        return ftAcc;
        };
    ;
ftN = 5; ftAcc = 1;
ftOut = 0;
ftOut = ft();
print "JRt2o interpreted : ftOut =" ftOut " (the oracle -- want 120)":;
print "JRt2o SENTINEL":;
stop();
